Roadmap
I broke it up into three overall phases based around concepts: Main scene, Sky Scene, Underwater Scene. 
Each phase can be broken into smaller manageable steps to ensure the path doesn't veer too far from my goal

# PHASE 1
Problem:
1. Lighting is too flat (biggest issue)

Everything is evenly lit → looks like a prototype

No strong shadows
No depth contrast
No mood shift

👉 This alone is the difference between:

“cool project”
“award-winning experience”

2. Materials feel placeholder
Sand, water, rocks = same “plastic” feel
No roughness variation
No micro detail

👉 Even stylized scenes need material richness

3. Environment lacks motion

Right now everything feels static

Water too uniform
Trees not reacting

👉 Award sites feel alive even when idle

4. UI breaks immersion
Up/down buttons feel default
Cards feel like overlay, not part of world
Typography isn’t integrated into 3D space

🚀 How to make it AWWWARD-WORTHY
Do this immediately:

1. Upgrade lighting to cinematic
Add:
Warm key light (sun)
Cool fill light (sky bounce)
Strong rim light on avatar
Effects:
Soft shadows (PCF or VSM)
Subtle fog / atmospheric perspective
Slight color gradient in sky
Distance fog (slight blue fade)
Haze between layers
Light rays (very subtle)

👉 You want:

“Pixar meets WebGL”

Right now:

Feels like mode switching
Not like time passing
Missing:
Smooth transitions between lighting states
Stronger contrast (especially sunset)
More directional lighting

👉 It still reads as “three presets” instead of a system

2. Add subtle environmental animation - the sky is too flat (especially daytime)

This is LOW effort, HIGH impact:

Clouds slowly drifting
Palm trees slightly swaying
Water shader with:
small waves
reflection distortion
Sun rotating slightly over scroll

Solid blue = no depth
Clouds don’t integrate with lighting

Make the sky dynamic
Gradient sky (top darker, horizon lighter)
Clouds:
Slight movement
Color changes with time of day
Sun glow / moon glow bloom

👉 Sky should be doing 50% of the visual work
👉 This makes the world feel alive

3. Fix materials (quick wins)

Sand → add:
gradient (wet near water)
subtle noise texture
Rocks → slight color variation
Water → Fresnel + reflection
Wood dock → roughness variation

Water feels like a placeholder
Repetitive pattern
No reflection of sky/sun/moon
No interaction with island

Add:
Darker sand near water (wetness gradient)
Stronger contact shadows under objects
Slight AO baked look
👉 Keep stylized, but not flat

4. Micro-interactions everywhere
Hover → slight scale + glow
Click → bounce / squash
Scroll → parallax layers

👉 Judges LOVE this detail

5. Make scroll feel physical

Right now scroll = navigation
You want scroll = force applied to world

Examples:
Scroll down → island slightly tilts
Camera eases forward (not linear)
Clouds move past faster as you “ascend”

👉 Add inertia + easing curves

6. Rebuild the UI into the world - UI still breaks immersion

Lighting toggle looks like a dev control
Up/down buttons still generic
Text feels “on top” instead of “in world”

Do:
Ideas:
Lighting toggle = floating orb near sky
Scroll indicator = wind current / arrow in environment
Buttons = carved into wood / glowing stones

Turn lighting toggle into an EXPERIENCE
Instead of:

click → instant switch

Do:

Smooth time interpolation (1–2 seconds)
Sun physically moves across sky
Sky color gradient shifts
Shadows rotate
Fire intensity increases at night
Bonus:
Add subtle sound transitions
birds → wind → night ambience

7. Sound design (massive differentiator)

Almost nobody does this well:

Ocean waves ambient
Wind in sky section
Underwater muffled sound
Subtle UI clicks

👉 This alone can elevate you into top-tier

8. Polish checklist (what wins awards)
No jank (60 FPS minimum)
Perfect easing curves
Consistent color grading
Cohesive transitions between sections

What you need:
❗ Lighting + motion + polish

